705:
(a) General. Permits for special events, and for the operation of a concession by a vendor at such a special event, in parks and recreation areas shall be obtained by application to the approving commissioner or the commissioner's designee in accordance with the following procedure:
(1) Application. A person seeking issuance of a permit hereunder shall file an application (with an application fee in an amount as established by the mayor and city council from time to time) stating:
a. The name and address of the applicant;
b. The name and address of the person, corporation or association sponsoring the activity, if any;
c. The day and hours for which the permit is desired;
d. The park or portion thereof for which the permit is desired;
e. The names and qualifications of the security company or individual law enforcement personnel whom the applicant intends to utilize to provide security for such event in accordance with section 20-24(c);
f. The expected attendance at such special event, including the maximum total attendance for the event, the average daily attendance for the event and the expected highest attendance for each day;
g. A copy of a rental agreement or other documentation evidencing:
1. That portable toilet or restroom facilities will be available at any special event where more than 150 people are expected to attend at any given time; and
2. That at least one such portable toilets will be provided at no cost to the city for each 150 people expected in attendance at any given time during such special event;
h. Any other information reasonably necessary to a determination as to whether a permit should be issued hereunder;
i. Variances required from park rules and regulations.
(2) Standards for issuance of a use permit. Standards for issuance of a use permit shall include the following findings:
a. That the proposed activity or use of the park will not unreasonably interfere with or detract from the general public's enjoyment of the park;
b. That the proposed activity and use will not unreasonably interfere with or detract from the promotion of public health, welfare, safety and recreation;
c. That the proposed activity or uses that are reasonably anticipated will not include violence, crime or disorderly conduct;
d. That the proposed activity will not entail extraordinary or burdensome expense or police operation by the city;
e. That the facilities desired have not been reserved for other use on the date and hour requested in the application.
(b) Appeal.
(1) Within ten days after the receipt of an application, the commissioner shall tell an applicant in writing of his decision to grant or deny a permit; in the event of a denial, the notification shall include the reason for the denial. Any aggrieved person shall have the right to appeal to the city council by serving written notice thereof on the city clerk within five working days of the refusal.
(2) A copy of the notice shall also be served on the recreation commission within the same time and the commission shall immediately forward the application and the reasons for its refusal to the city council. The city council shall decide within ten days from the receipt of the appeal by the city clerk, or at its first meeting after the appeal, whichever is later. The decision of the city council shall be final.
(c) Bound by park rules. A permittee shall be bound by all park rules and regulations and all applicable ordinances fully as though the same were inserted in the permits.
(d) Liability insurance; exceptions. Except for wedding parties, pre-school, elementary and secondary school-sponsored activities and public library events, every applicant for a permit shall be required to submit evidence of liability insurance covering injuries to members of the general public arising out of such permitted activities in the amount of $1,000,000.00 or such higher amount as may be from time to time determined prior to the commencement of any activity or issuance of any permit.
(e) Parade. Any group must have a permit for a parade.
(f) Revocation. The city council shall have the authority to revoke a permit upon a finding of violation of any rule or ordinance or upon good cause shown.
(Code 2003, § 93.34; Ord. of 4-15-2002)

713:
